##
Leaving a loop early, and skipping a turn.

'break' leaves the innermost loop it is inside. 'continue' abandons the rest of this turn
and goes on to the next one. Both are ways of saying "I am done here" without arranging the
condition to say it for you.

Neither is necessary — every loop below could be written with a flag and a longer
condition — but that arrangement is harder to read than the sentence it replaces, and the
flag is one more thing to keep true.
##

global model Program

    function Main()
        Program.StoppingAtTheFirstMatch();
        Program.SkippingWhatDoesNotCount();
        Program.BothAtOnce();
        Program.BreakingOutOfTheInnerLoop();
    end function

    # ---- Stopping once the answer is known -----------------------------------------

    ##
    Once the first even number is found there is nothing left to learn, so the loop stops
    rather than walking the rest of the set to no purpose.

    The result is an optional because the set may hold no even number at all. That is the
    honest answer, and it is why nothing here has to invent a -1 to mean "not found".
    ##
    integer? function FirstEven(integer[] values)
        integer? found;

        for each value in values
            if value % 2 == 0
                found = value;
                break;
            end if
        end for

        yield found;
    end function

    function StoppingAtTheFirstMatch()
        integer? even = Program.FirstEven({7, 3, 12, 5, 8});
        Console.WriteLine("first even: " + even.Or(0));

        integer? none = Program.FirstEven({7, 3, 5});
        Console.WriteLine("none found: " + none.HasValue());
    end function

    # ---- Skipping the turns that do not count ----------------------------------------

    ##
    A refund is recorded as a negative amount and is not part of the day's takings. The
    'continue' says so once, at the top, instead of wrapping the rest of the body in an if.
    ##
    integer function TakingsFrom(integer[] amounts)
        integer total = 0;

        for each amount in amounts
            if amount < 0
                continue;
            end if

            total = total + amount;
        end for

        yield total;
    end function

    function SkippingWhatDoesNotCount()
        Console.WriteLine("takings: " + Program.TakingsFrom({40, -15, 60, 25, -10}));
    end function

    # ---- Both in one loop -------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
    A range loop works the same way. 'continue' still moves the counter on, so this cannot
    loop forever the way a hand-written 'while' with a misplaced continue can.
    ##
    function BothAtOnce()
        integer counted = 0;

        for i = 1 to 20
            # Odd numbers are not what we are counting.
            if i % 2 == 1
                continue;
            end if

            counted = counted + 1;

            # Four is enough.
            if counted == 4
                Console.WriteLine("stopped at " + i);
                break;
            end if
        end for
    end function

    # ---- Which loop a break leaves ---------------------------------------------------

    ##
    A break leaves the innermost loop and no more. The outer loop below runs every one of
    its turns; only the inner one is cut short each time.
    ##
    function BreakingOutOfTheInnerLoop()
        for row = 1 to 3
            string line = "";

            for column = 1 to 5
                if column > row
                    break;
                end if

                line = line + column + " ";
            end for

            Console.WriteLine("row " + row + ": " + line);
        end for
    end function

end model
